WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.243
Can you watch the cerebellum learn?

2
00:00:06.103 --> 00:00:07.623
Put on prism goggles, and throw.

3
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:10.813
The goggles shift what you see to the right.

4
00:00:11.493 --> 00:00:13.253
So the first throws miss to the right.

5
00:00:14.216 --> 00:00:15.716
Each miss teaches a little.

6
00:00:16.696 --> 00:00:19.116
Throw by throw, the misses walk in toward the target.

7
00:00:20.116 --> 00:00:23.196
How much each error teaches sets how fast this curve falls.

8
00:00:23.976 --> 00:00:26.856
Call it k, the fraction of the error corrected per throw.

9
00:00:27.866 --> 00:00:29.386
Now take the goggles off.

10
00:00:30.266 --> 00:00:31.366
Where does the next throw land?

11
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.560
To the left. The learned correction is still being applied, to a world that has

12
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.120
changed back. That opposite miss is the after-effect.

13
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:51.163
It shows that the mapping between seeing and moving was recalibrated.

14
00:00:52.116 --> 00:00:56.916
Real after-effects are usually smaller, partly because people drop a deliberate

15
00:00:56.916 --> 00:00:59.316
aiming strategy once the goggles are off.

16
00:01:00.266 --> 00:01:05.526
In people with damage to the olivocerebellar system, adaptation at this task was

17
00:01:05.526 --> 00:01:06.526
impaired.

18
00:01:07.016 --> 00:01:09.916
In the model, a smaller k means smaller steps.

19
00:01:10.756 --> 00:01:15.116
The curve flattens, and after the same number of throws the after-effect is smaller

20
00:01:15.116 --> 00:01:18.376
too. The model does not say which part is damaged.

21
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.380
One account: lose the cerebellum's prediction, and the arm leans on slow feedback.

22
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.540
Movements can overshoot and swing near the target.

23
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:33.233
Now the side. A patient has a lesion in the left cerebellar hemisphere.

24
00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:36.133
Which arm is more likely to show tremor?

25
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:47.833
The left arm. Cerebellar output crosses to the right thalamus, which relays to the

26
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:48.833
right motor cortex.

27
00:01:49.866 --> 00:01:54.506
Then the corticospinal tract crosses back, to the left side of the spinal cord.

28
00:01:55.326 --> 00:01:56.326
Two crossings cancel.

29
00:01:57.466 --> 00:02:01.046
The takeaway: the cerebellum helps learn predictions from error.

30
00:02:02.226 --> 00:02:06.566
Damage can leave a limb leaning more on late feedback, one cause of clumsiness, on

31
00:02:06.566 --> 00:02:07.246
the same side.
